{
  peppy_schema: "contract/v1",
  manifest: {
    name: "simulation_robot",
    tag: "v1",
    description: [
      "One robot in a shared simulation. A robot joins the scene by attaching, naming the copy it runs as, the model it is, and where it stands, and it stays for as long as its attach goal runs.",
      "The goal never completes. A cancel by the caller takes the robot out and ends the goal cancelled. When the engine takes the robot out itself, it fails the goal with `resource_unavailable`, and the message says why.",
      "Nothing here carries motion. A robot's limbs are paired to the engine directly, one pair per limb over joint_link or gripper_link, and the copy the engine reads on each pair is the one this goal named: that is how a setpoint reaches the robot it was meant for and how its measured state comes back on the same pair.",
      "An engine holds every robot's pairs on one slot per kind of pair, so nothing in a slot's name says which limb a pair drives: the limb is the name of the link the pair comes from on the robot's side. A robot therefore names the links it pairs to an engine after its limbs, the names it answers to in limb_motion and limb_state (left_arm, arm, gripper), which are the names this contract lists for its model. No schema holds that rule. A link named otherwise still pairs, and the engine finds it in the match. The pair grace time of the engine is how long it keeps a robot whose pairs are not the ones its model has. A robot whose pairs name a limb its model lacks, or miss one its model has, for longer than that time does not stay: the engine takes it out, and the message of the failed goal names both lists. A robot whose pairs all go away for longer than that time does not stay either.",
      "Whether the engine is standing a robot and holding a pair for every limb its model has is that robot's readiness, which the engine answers on is_ready to the instance that attached it.",
    ],
  },
  failure_kinds: {
    busy: "A robot in the way of the goal still joins or leaves the scene; the same goal can succeed once that robot stands or is out",
  },
  interfaces: {
    services: [
      {
        name: "is_ready",
        description: "Tell whether the asking instance's robot is ready to be driven: it stands in the scene and the engine holds a pair for every limb its model has, so a setpoint reaches each limb and its measured state comes back. False for an instance that stands no robot here.",
        response_message_format: {
          ready: "bool",
        },
      },
    ],
    actions: [
      {
        name: "attach",
        description: "Join the scene. The goal is accepted when the model is in the engine's catalogue, the name can stand beside the robots of the scene and of the goals the engine already accepted, and the engine can promise a placement: the one the goal names, or a free spot of its own choosing. Otherwise the engine refuses it: with `rejected` for a model that is not in its catalogue or that it cannot stand, and for a name or a placement that it does not take; with `busy` while a robot in the way of the name still leaves the scene (the robot of the name, a robot whose name cannot stand beside it, or another robot of the caller), and while the robot of the name is the caller's own robot of the same model and still joins; and with `precondition_failed` for a name that a robot which stays keeps out, and for a placement it cannot promise. A goal refused with `busy` can succeed once the robot in its way is out or stands, so a caller sends it again; any other refusal stays until someone changes the scene. The engine then builds the robot, places it, sends one feedback message, standing true, once the robot stands, and runs the goal for as long as the robot is in the scene. A robot that the engine cannot stand once it accepted the goal fails the goal: with `attempt_failed` when the robot's files do not come or its model does not build, and with `precondition_failed` when the place it promised is no longer free. An engine that cannot send that feedback takes the robot out and fails the goal with `resource_unavailable`. A caller holds one robot: a second attach naming another robot while its first stands or joins is refused with `precondition_failed`, and so is one naming another model for the robot of the name; while that robot still leaves, both are refused with `busy`. A caller attaching the robot it already stands is registering that robot again, which is what an instance that died and came back does. The engine admits it and hands the robot's stay to the new goal: the robot is never stood twice and never leaves, and the goal that held it ends cancelled, with a reason that says another goal of the same caller holds the robot now. The sender grace of the action is the pair grace time: when the process that sent the goal is gone, the engine keeps its robot for that time before the goal receives its cancel, so an instance that comes back within it registers the robot that still stands. The feedback stream ends with the goal, which is how the caller learns the stay is over without asking. The result is empty.",
        goal_service: {
          request_message_format: {
            robot: { $type: "string", $description: "The copy this robot runs as, which is the name it stands under and the name the engine reads on every one of its limb pairs. A robot launched outside a copy names itself." },
            model: { $type: "string", $description: "An id of the engine's robot catalogue, for example openarm_v2." },
            placement: {
              $type: "object",
              $optional: true,
              $description: "Where the robot's base stands on the floor. Absent, the engine places the robot on a free spot of its own choosing.",
              position: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 3, $description: "World-frame x, y, z in metres." },
              yaw: { $type: "f64", $description: "Rotation about the world's +z in radians." },
            },
          },
          response_message_format: {
            arm_names: { $type: "array", $items: "string", $description: "The model's arms, under the names the robot answers to in limb_motion and limb_state, which are the names of the links its arm pairs come from." },
            arm_joints: { $type: "array", $items: "u32", $description: "Joints of each arm, index-aligned with arm_names: how many values that arm's pair carries." },
            gripper_names: { $type: "array", $items: "string", $description: "The model's grippers, named the same way. A gripper has a name of its own, whichever arm carries it." },
          },
        },
        feedback_topic: {
          qos_profile: "standard",
          message_format: {
            standing: "bool",
          },
        },
        result_service: {},
        failure_kinds: ["busy"],
      },
    ],
  },
}
